{
  "_about": "Opening hours of community centres and older-adult centres from city open data, read by mapsee_ingest_facility_hours.py: one STANDING row per centre (recurring_days), plus dated rows for Seattle's Teen Late Night programme. Each source's `osm` map is written by `--propose-osm` and checked by a person: mode `same` means this adapter writes the OSM community_centre listing's own row (osm_amenities skips that element, see claimed_osm_refs), `colocated` means another centre in the same building, drawn on the same dot. A centre this adapter owns whose hours it refuses today (closed, no hours for the season, unreadable, or gone from the city's data) is handled by mapsee_ingest_facility_hours.refused_event: a taken-over listing is REWRITTEN as a listing with no hours, never skipped; an own row is not written (as pin_only scenery ../mapsee still opened a sheet with a 24-hour clock), so an own row written once and refused later keeps its last week until a retirement exists. Seattle's `summer` window ends on Labor Day because the centres' own pages say 'Fall 2026 (begins Sept 8)'; its June start is not measured (the pages show only the current season). `late_night_days_ahead` keeps the programme's projected evenings short, because an upsert cannot remove one the open data later drops. Washington, DC publishes no hours in open data (2026-10-05: DPR's 70 recreation centres, the 114 aging-services sites, 28 libraries and 49 group meal centres on maps2.dcgis.dc.gov carry none), so DC has no source here.",
  "late_night_days_ahead": 21,
  "max_age_days": 400,
